---
import PageLayout from "@/layouts/PageLayout.astro";
import Prose from "@/components/astro/Prose.astro";
import { DISCLAIMER } from "@/lib/site";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
---

<PageLayout
  title="Chat Statistics — Every Message Statistic Your Chat Export Can Give You"
  description="From message counts and activity heatmaps to response times and emoji rankings: a practical overview of the chat statistics you can compute from WhatsApp and Telegram exports — privately, in your browser."
  path="/chat-statistics/"
>
  <Prose />
  <main id="main" class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-12">
    <h1 class="font-display text-4xl font-bold text-white">Chat Statistics, Explained</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-zinc-300">
      A chat export is a small dataset with surprisingly rich structure. This page walks through the
      statistics that can be computed honestly from a WhatsApp or Telegram export — and what each
      one can and cannot tell you. ChatWrapped computes all of them locally in your browser.
    </p>
    <a
      href={`${base}/app/`}
      class="mt-8 inline-block rounded-2xl bg-gradient-to-r from-violet-600 to-fuchsia-600 px-7 py-3.5 text-base font-bold text-white shadow-xl shadow-fuchsia-950/40 transition hover:scale-[1.02]"
    >
      Compute my chat statistics →
    </a>

    <div class="prose-cw">
      <h2>Volume statistics</h2>
      <p>
        The foundation: <strong>eligible message counts</strong>, per participant and overall; <strong
          >word counts</strong
        >
        (locale-aware tokenization, including stopwords); <strong>character counts</strong> (counted as
        grapheme clusters, so emoji and non-Latin scripts count as what they look like); and <strong
          >average message length</strong
        >. Good tools separate system messages and attachment placeholders from these totals —
        ChatWrapped shows both raw and eligible numbers side by side.
      </p>

      <h2>Distribution statistics</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Message share</strong> per participant (their messages ÷ all eligible messages) answers
        “who texts more”. Shares are most meaningful in 1:1 and small groups; in a 50-person group the
        long tail tells its own story — which is why per-participant tables beat pie charts at scale.
      </p>

      <h2>Time statistics</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Monthly and daily trends</strong> — the conversation’s seasons and spikes.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Hour × weekday heatmap</strong> — when the chat is alive; night owls show up immediately.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Weekday vs weekend share</strong> — work-chat vs weekend-chat energy.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Active-day streaks</strong> — the longest run of consecutive days with at least one
          message.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Busiest day / month</strong> — records with their full dates.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Timezone caveat: exports store local wall-clock time without timezone metadata. ChatWrapped
        preserves what the sender saw on their clock and computes durations correctly; it never
        invents a timezone.
      </p>

      <h2>Response statistics</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Response time</strong> needs rules to be honest: ChatWrapped groups rapid consecutive
        messages by the same person into a “burst”, measures a response from the burst’s <em
          >first</em
        > message to the first message from anyone else, and only counts responses within six hours for
        averages. Median is preferred over mean — one overnight silence shouldn’t dominate. Full rules
        in the
        <a href={`${base}/how-chat-analysis-works/`}>metric guide</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Text statistics</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Emoji ranking</strong> — counted as grapheme clusters so 👍🏽 and 👨‍👩‍👧 are one emoji each;
          ©/®/™ excluded.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Signature words</strong> — ranked after removing stopwords, numbers, URLs and email
          addresses.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Question and exclamation rates</strong> — small but surprisingly characterful numbers.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What chat statistics cannot tell you</h2>
      <p>
        They are counting statistics. They cannot measure affection, loyalty, attention or
        “toxicity”, and they say nothing about what happens between messages. Anyone claiming
        otherwise is selling something. ChatWrapped’s stories and awards are deliberately framed as
        playful activity facts.
      </p>

      <h2>Compute them privately</h2>
      <p>
        ChatWrapped calculates every statistic above from your export in your browser — no upload,
        no account. Start from the <a href={`${base}/app/`}>analyzer</a> or read the export guides for
        <a href={`${base}/how-to-export-whatsapp-chat/`}>WhatsApp</a> and
        <a href={`${base}/how-to-export-telegram-chat/`}>Telegram</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-10 text-xs leading-relaxed text-zinc-600">{DISCLAIMER}</p>
  </main>
</PageLayout>
